---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { site, url } from '../data/site';
import Icon from '../components/Icon.astro';
---
<Base title="About">
  <div class="container section" style="max-width:860px">
    <div class="card about">
      <img class="about-img" src={url('avatar.png')} alt="SurGamingOnInsulin's GitHub avatar" width="200" height="200" />
      <div class="prose">
        <h1 style="margin-top:0">About me</h1>
        <p><strong>Sur Gaming On Insulin</strong> · Netherlands</p>
        <p>
          I am a creator who enjoys turning ideas into useful, downloadable projects. I build Minecraft
          resource packs, data packs, plugins and mods, and I am also experimenting with small tools such as
          the Music Converter.
        </p>
        <p>
          I like projects that are practical, easy to use and shared openly. This site is my workshop: a place
          to keep my releases together, explain how they work, and make it simple for people to try them.
        </p>
        <div class="actions">
          <a class="btn primary" href={`mailto:${site.email}?subject=Hello%20from%20Pixelith`}><Icon name="mail" size={18} /> Email me</a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="card prose" style="margin-top:1rem">
      <h2 style="margin-top:0">Installing a project</h2>
      <p>Not sure where a download goes? Here is the quick version. Most projects come as a <code>.zip</code> or <code>.jar</code>; keep the file as it is unless the project says otherwise.</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Resource pack:</strong> leave the <code>.zip</code> zipped, place it in <code>.minecraft/resourcepacks</code>, then select it in Minecraft under <em>Options → Resource Packs</em>.</li>
        <li><strong>Data pack:</strong> place the <code>.zip</code> in <code>saves/&lt;your-world&gt;/datapacks</code>. In the world, run <code>/reload</code> or re-enter the world.</li>
        <li><strong>Plugin:</strong> put the <code>.jar</code> in your Paper, Spigot, Purpur or Folia server's <code>plugins</code> folder, then restart the server.</li>
        <li><strong>Mod:</strong> first install the loader named on the project page (Fabric, Forge, NeoForge or Quilt), then put the <code>.jar</code> in <code>.minecraft/mods</code>.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>If something does not appear, check that Minecraft, the loader and the project all support the same version. You can use the GitHub link on a project page to report a problem or ask for help.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</Base>
